WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.810 --> 00:05.000
Our pianist, Paula, works on the inventory team at the warehouse,

2
00:05.000 --> 00:10.600
and her supervisor has asked her to list out all equipment that needs
repairs or maintenance so that she can coordinate repairs.

3
00:10.600 --> 00:15.100
While Paula understands the project is necessary,
it just doesn't excite her.

4
00:15.270 --> 00:18.310
She even worries she could fail,
given how little she knows about it.

5
00:18.790 --> 00:26.400
But as soon as she recognizes that this is an opportunity to decrease the stress
for her and her coworkers caused by broken equipment, her motivation increases.

6
00:26.400 --> 00:35.130
With a clear link to something that is personally meaningful, the well-being of her team,
the task at hand feels less daunting and more worthwhile.

7
00:37.030 --> 00:39.750
So Paula sets a clear learning intention for herself:

8
00:40.810 --> 00:46.110
be able to list out all equipment in need of repairs,
so that both she and her team can make an informed decision.

9
00:48.710 --> 00:56.740
She sets up time to talk to her coworkers to better understand which equipment
requires repairs, and then does further research to document the results.

10
00:57.510 --> 01:04.900
The more she learns about which equipment needs repairs, the more ways she sees
how this can improve her team members' lives, which in turn energizes her.

11
01:05.700 --> 01:09.120
When Paula takes the pressure off herself
to offer a quick recommendation,

12
01:09.930 --> 01:12.800
and recognizes she should spend time learning and exploring,

13
01:13.510 --> 01:15.710
the task becomes easier and more fun.

14
01:16.550 --> 01:24.260
After a few weeks, and with her team's input, she is able to develop a full list
of equipment and help her supervisor submit a complete repair order.

15
01:26.570 --> 01:34.050
The research is clear that performing in the long run requires you to release
performance goals in the early stages, in order to focus on your learning.

16
01:35.210 --> 01:37.600
And the research is clear about something else, too:

17
01:38.210 --> 01:41.310
that the best learning intentions
are the ones that are linked to meaning.

18
01:42.950 --> 01:46.310
When we have to learn something difficult,
and over a longer period of time,

19
01:46.950 --> 01:49.030
it's critical to be intrinsically motivated.

20
01:50.120 --> 01:55.110
Think about the last time you engaged in an activity
that you truly enjoyed and that was personally rewarding.

21
01:56.330 --> 02:00.640
There was no prize, threat, or external factor
influencing your behavior,

22
02:01.540 --> 02:03.810
only this heartwarming feeling inside of you.

23
02:04.650 --> 02:06.180
That's intrinsic motivation.

24
02:07.010 --> 02:10.050
It makes us feel more empowered
and in charge of our experience,

25
02:10.850 --> 02:13.440
because it touches on our deep human need for autonomy:

26
02:14.310 --> 02:16.190
to choose where we focus our energy.

27
02:16.970 --> 02:22.310
Being able to see the task at hand as connected to something
important to you is a powerfully different way to learn.

28
02:24.170 --> 02:30.500
In Paula's case, her ability to make an explicit connection between doing a good job
of creating the equipment repair list and realizing it would improve things for her team

29
02:30.500 --> 02:36.900
motivated her brain to focus and engage deeply
in the activities involved.

30
02:37.830 --> 02:42.820
In turn, this makes learning easier, and you are
more likely to successfully achieve your intention.
